  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      This is a short essay about the Middle East between the world wars, seen
      through the Circassian diaspora. Amin Samkough is named in <strong>one
      sentence</strong>. It is also someone else’s published work, so I have
      <strong>not</strong> translated it in full. This page summarizes it in my
      own words and translates a few short passages. Please have a Turkish
      reader check the translations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of
      or that disagrees with another source on this site.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The setting.</strong> When the Ottoman Empire fell, many Arabs
        expected an Arab kingdom on the basis of self-determination, as in
        Woodrow Wilson’s Fourteen Points. Instead, after the 1919 Paris Peace
        Conference, almost all Arabic-speaking lands outside central and
        southern Arabia were ruled by colonial regimes. France set up republics
        in Syria and Lebanon, and Britain set up constitutional monarchies in
        Iraq and Transjordan (<span lang="tr">«Mavera-i Ürdün»</span>, literally
        “beyond the Jordan”). Palestine was the exception.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The tension.</strong> Between the wars, state-based nationalism
        (<span lang="tr">«vataniye»</span>) pulled against pan-Arabism
        (<span lang="tr">«kavmiye»</span>). The author says the new states
        became permanent in the 1940s and 1950s, yet ordinary people, especially
        urban professionals and the urban poor, kept seeking a solution in
        pan-Arabism.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The Circassians.</strong> He asks how the Middle Eastern
        Circassian diaspora, split among five places (Syria, Transjordan,
        Palestine, Egypt and Iraq), fared in these years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The KDHP.</strong> He calls the Caucasus Mountaineers’ People’s
        Party (<span lang="tr">«Kafkasya Dağlıları Halk Partisi»</span>, KDHP)
        the “guiding mind” of the diaspora in the 1920s and 1930s. It was
        founded in Czechoslovakia in 1926 and soon moved to Poland. Its aim was
        to unite the “old” (1864–1878) and the “new” (1921) emigrations. Affiliated
        associations were set up in Syria (1928), Transjordan (1932) and Egypt
        (1932), and Syria was “the driving force” among the five centres.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Syria.</strong> The one mention of Samkough, quoted below.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Transjordan.</strong> The Amman Circassians’ story was different.
        The regime won public acceptance and rested on four groups: the
        “King’s men”, urban merchants, farmers and a Bedouin army. The author
        says Circassians were mainly in the farmer and army groups.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Egypt, Palestine, Iraq.</strong> Egypt came to terms with an
        Arab identity from the mid-1930s. The small Circassian villages of Kfar
        Kama and Reyhaniye in Mandate Palestine kept out of the Jewish–Arab
        conflict. Iraq, with Chechen, Daghestani and Circassian communities, was
        a stronghold of Arab nationalism, nicknamed “the Prussia of the Arab
        world”.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Conclusion.</strong> The post-1918 order cut both ways for
        Circassians: it divided their colonies among different states, but it
        also strengthened state-based nationalisms and so weakened the pan-Arab
        current that might otherwise have targeted them. He argues the period
        needs careful comparative study.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
