  <section id="samkough">
    <h2>What it says about Amin Samkough</h2>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="tr">
        Fransız Mandası’nın getirdiği özgürlüklerden yararlanan Emin Semguğ
        önderliğindeki Çerkesler bilhassa en yoğun bulundukları yer olan Golan
        Tepeleri’nde kültürel bir uyanış yaşadılar.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          “Under the leadership of Emin Semguğ, and making use of the freedoms
          the French Mandate brought, the Circassians experienced a cultural
          awakening, especially in the Golan Heights, where they were most
          densely settled.”
        </em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      The article gives him no dates, no title and no further detail, and it
      does not mention his society, his newspaper or the school.
    </p>
    <p>For context, here is the sentence that sets up the Syrian paragraph:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p lang="tr">
        Bu beş ulusal merkez içinde itici güç Suriye idi.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>“Among these five national centres, the driving force was Syria.”</em>
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>Things to review</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the link between Samkough and the KDHP is implied,
          not stated.</strong> The article says KDHP-affiliated associations
          were founded in Syria in 1928, and then, in a separate paragraph,
          speaks of Samkough’s leadership. It never says he led or belonged to
          the KDHP’s Syrian branch, and it never names the Syrian association.
          Your
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/north-caucasians-in-emigration/">2020 dictionary entry</a>
          does record him as a board member of the party, but that entry gives
          no source. More on the party and its sources is on the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/caucasus-mountaineers-peoples-party-resources/">resources page</a>.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>1927 or 1928.</strong> Mirzoev dates the Syrian
          society’s founding to 1927 and its published charter to 1928. This
          article gives 1928 for the Syrian KDHP-affiliated association. Are they
          the same body? The article doesn’t say.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>where the party was based.</strong> The article says
          it was founded in Czechoslovakia in 1926 and moved to Poland. The
          dictionary entry page on this site says other entries in that book
          place the party in Paris and Istanbul. Those may be different phases
          or different offices, or one source may be wrong.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the spelling “Emin Semguğ”.</strong> This is a third
          form on this site, beside “Samkough Emin” in Jineps’s 2022 article and
          “Samgug Amin” in the Russian-based versions. It is the Turkish
          spelling of the same name, but the page doesn’t say so.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>“cultural awakening”.</strong> This is the author’s
          characterization, with no reference. It fits the rest of the site, but
          the article offers no evidence for it.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the party’s end date.</strong> The note under the
          article gives the party’s years as 1926–1939, but the text never
          mentions 1939 or says why it ended then.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="review">
    <h2>General caution about this article</h2>
    <p>
      This is an interpretive essay with <strong>no footnotes or references</strong>.
      The wider history it relies on has not been checked here. Statements that
      read as the author’s own argument, not as established fact, include:
    </p>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: that the post-1919 state system “brought only destruction” to
          the Arabs, and that Palestine had no common state because of favoritism
          toward the Jews (<span lang="tr">«Yahudilerin kayrılması»</span>).
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: that Egypt left Egyptian nationalism behind from the mid-1930s,
          leading to Nasser in the 1950s.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: that Circassians were “mainly” farmers and soldiers in
          Transjordan. The author hedges it himself (“it would be right to
          think”), so it’s an inference.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: that the post-1918 order weakened a pan-Arab wind that was
          “likely” to target Circassian colonies. The author says “likely”, so
          this is speculation.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="translation-notes">
    <h2>Translation notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        “Westphalia order” in the article means the modern system of sovereign
        nation-states. <span lang="tr">«Vataniye»</span> and
        <span lang="tr">«kavmiye»</span> are Turkish renderings of the Arabic
        terms <em>wataniyya</em> (loyalty to a particular country) and
        <em>qawmiyya</em> (pan-Arab nationalism).
      </li>
      <li>
        I translated the two quoted sentences myself. They are short and plain,
        but a Turkish reader should still check them.
      </li>
    </ul>
